course: dictionary
lesson: 4818
title: Hungover and worse for wear
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Hungover is an informal word. It describes how someone feels the morning after drinking too much alcohol: ill, tired and with a headache. «He felt hungover and stayed in bed until lunch.» Hungover is the adjective. A hangover is the noun, the headache and sick feeling itself.
explain: Context helps you spot the meaning. Words like sick, sleepy and thirsty tell you the body is unwell. Try your meaning in the sentence: after drinking too much, feeling ill and tired makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Britons also have other ways of saying it. «Feeling delicate» is a polite way to say hungover, and «worse for wear» means drunk or badly hungover. Polite words can hide the real meaning, so read the whole sentence to check.
explain: Health lessons use this word to show that too much alcohol harms the body. Knowing what hungover means helps you understand why looking after your body matters.
fact: Hungover is an adjective: «He felt hungover.»
fact: A hangover is the noun: the headache and sick feeling the morning after drinking too much alcohol.
fact: «Hungover» means feeling ill, tired and with a headache after drinking too much alcohol the night before.
fact: «Feeling delicate» is a polite British way to say hungover.
fact: «Worse for wear» means drunk or badly hungover.
exercise: What does hungover mean? | Feeling bright and full of energy | Feeling cold after swimming in the sea | *Feeling ill and tired with a headache after drinking too much alcohol the night before | Feeling very hungry after a long walk
exercise: The text says «He is feeling delicate this morning.» What does delicate mean here? | *A polite way to say hungover | Careful and gentle with others | Clean and tidy | Very thin and fragile
exercise: Which word is the noun form of hungover? | *hangover | hungry | hung | overdo
test: The text says «She was worse for wear after the party.» What does worse for wear mean? | *Drunk or badly hungover | Tidier than before | Happy to see her friends | Hungry for dinner
test: Which sentence uses hangover as a noun? | He was very hungry. | She felt hungover at school. | *The hangover made her head pound. | They went to the park.
exam: «After the party, Sam felt ___ and slept until noon.» Which word fits best? | luminous | treacherous | *hungover | famished
